A||||10||EdsB52||Getrag trannies||||1||
Z||000000||EdsB52||06-25-2001||02:14 PM||||Well guys, I have good news and bad news. First, I have been given information that it won't happen for us to change the final drive gear ratio with the Getrag. I researched and called three transmision shops that specialize in manual shift trannies. They told me it would cost thousands to have the final drive gears manufactured and that there's no other way. This all but eliminates my desire to transplant an LT-1/LS-1. It seems the 3.4TDC would be the best match for that transmission.<P>Now for the good news; I found a shop that rebuilds Getrags. Their addres is: <A HREF="http://www.tbtrans.com/GPpage4.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tbtrans.com/GPpage4.htm</A> <P>They're in Spokane, Washington and they said it costs $125 + parts.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Will||06-25-2001||02:38 PM||||That page seems to be for the Getrag 290, which is a longitudinal transmission (notice the designation 5LM60--L for longitudinal, M for manual, 5 for 5 speed, 60 for the torque rating, but not in foot lbs)<P>Do they also do the 5TM60 (T for transverse) or Getrag 282, which is the transmission we use?<P>You're going to give up well over 100 HP at the flywheel because you don't like the gearing?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||06-25-2001||02:50 PM||||I don't know why the final drive ratio would cause you to change your mind about engines.<P>Top speed is the least important as far as performance goes. How often does someone get the opportunity to go over 120?<P>How fast you can get to 120 or 100 or 60 is what's important.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||06-25-2001||10:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sjp777:<BR><B>I don't know why the final drive ratio would cause you to change your mind about engines.<P>Top speed is the least important as far as performance goes. How often does someone get the opportunity to go over 120?<P>How fast you can get to 120 or 100 or 60 is what's important.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Top speed is least important to you, you mean. How about sustaining 80-90mph for long periods? Do you want to wear out your motor for nothing? I used to top out my GSXR's all the time; 170mph indicated.||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||06-25-2001||11:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>That page seems to be for the Getrag 290, which is a longitudinal transmission (notice the designation 5LM60--L for longitudinal, M for manual, 5 for 5 speed, 60 for the torque rating, but not in foot lbs)<P>Do they also do the 5TM60 (T for transverse) or Getrag 282, which is the transmission we use?<P>You're going to give up well over 100 HP at the flywheel because you don't like the gearing?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>They said they rebuild Fiero Getrags. He said he was familiar with them and that they are pretty rare.<P>Actually, someone wouldn't have to give up that much (over 100 flywheel HP). The 3.4 TDC puts out 215 vs the LT-1's 275, or LS-1's 315. It's the torque that I would miss more than the HP. But I also wouldn't miss the weight. With the Fiero's geometry and transmission, I've concluded the 3.4 TDC is the best match for overall driving. Too bad it's a *****  to maintain.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lou_dias||06-26-2001||11:28 AM||||Why not get a Cavalier Getrag with the 3.42 final drive and I even heard of a guy with a lambo kit 4 years ago in Kitcar magazine that put in 3.23 gears.  Also check out <A HREF="http://www.isuzuperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.isuzuperformance.com</A> <BR>they may have lower gears...||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||06-26-2001||01:31 PM||||Ya they might have it. Wouldn't it be classic if the Isuzu is easily re-gearable and not the Getrag?! But let's keep this in context; my Z-28 has a tranny output of .50 in 6th gear, the diff is 3.42; conclusion, the final drive is 1.71, right? I'm looking for something close to that if not that exactly. Considering my Camaro is 7-800lbs heavier that the Fiero and the engine tranny pull the Camaro very well, I think it would be crazy to shorten the ratio at all. Nice web site, I'll check em out.||||reg||1
Z||000007||onfire||06-26-2001||01:38 PM||||Was checking out <A HREF="http://www.isuzuperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.isuzuperformance.com</A>  and found this.<P>Custom final drive ratio gear sets for any application <BR>Custom ring and pinoin or ring and output shaft sets.<P>Starting at $1,499.00, call for specific quotes.<P>Shipping on gear sets is by UPS and costs are as follows: Continental US, $16.00; Hawaii, Alaska, and Canada, $21.00; Outside these areas, please email for shipping costs. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||06-26-2001||01:40 PM||||What about the 3.94 getrag found in Beretta GTZ's?  It's rare but I am sure one can be found.||||reg||6
Z||000009||Toddster||06-26-2001||01:57 PM||||The 3.94 is heading in the wrong direction. Great for autocrossing but terrible if you want to go faster than 40 mph.<P>A 3.2 (or thereabouts) would be perfect. My biggest *****  with the Getrag has been first gear. You barely move and then you have to shift. Stop and go traffic is even worse.||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||06-26-2001||02:26 PM||||Actually Toddster I think that's quite a bit shy. Like I wrote; my tranny drive is .50 in 6th, the diff ratio is 3.42, doesn't that equate to 1.71? Someone correct me if I'm wrong. In fact, the 1993 Camaro was .062 in 6th and GM thought that was too shallow and steepened it up a bit to .50.  I will check into the possible ratios with a phone call to them. $1500 sounds like a lot, but when you figure the cost of the engine and untimely rebuild, it's nothing. Then you can factor in the savings of gas, reduction in noise, and overall improvement in performance and enjoyment in driving and realize the extra cost is negligible.||||reg||1
